## Dissertation paragraph (Feature Engineering)

Following data cleaning, the session-level dataset was transformed into a participant-level dataset through a feature engineering process. The original dataset contained repeated observations for each participant, with separate therapist and parent assessments recorded for each intervention session. As the objective of this study was to identify participant-level behavioural trajectories, the data were grouped by participant identifier, and summary statistics were calculated across all intervention sessions. The first engineered feature represented the number of intervention sessions completed by each participant. Because each session was recorded twice (once by a therapist and once by a parent), the number of sessions was calculated using the count of unique session numbers rather than the total number of records. This ensured that the feature accurately reflected the eight intervention sessions completed by each participant.

## Paragraph for Mean Engagement

A participant-level mean engagement score was subsequently calculated by averaging engagement ratings across all intervention sessions. This feature provides a summary measure of the participant's overall engagement during the storytelling intervention while reducing multiple session-level observations into a single representative value. The mean was calculated using the available observations for each participant, with missing values automatically excluded from the calculation. This approach preserved participants with incomplete observations while ensuring that the feature accurately represented their recorded engagement throughout the intervention.


